-- 精选内容英文版：建列 + 写入 19 条人工译文。
-- 由 Claude 逐条翻译（非机翻），2026-08-03。在 Supabase SQL Editor 里整份执行。

alter table public.student_history_records
  add column if not exists featured_en jsonb;

comment on column public.student_history_records.featured_en is
  'English translation of the publicly visible featured fields: {title, angle, coachFeedback, excerpt{...}}. Null means not translated yet — the reader falls back to the Chinese original.';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Net shots come from the knee, not the hand","angle":"\"Bend the knee to drive the waist and play the net shot quickly\" — turning the net from a hand job into a leg job. That is the right direction. And this one was written up two days late, on purpose.","excerpt":{"title":"Back-to-baseline review","reflection":"Catching up on the summary from the 24th. Worked on swing, movement and net shots — bending the knee to drive the waist into a quick net shot. On the clear, turning side-on and letting body momentum carry the arm. Stuck on: needs more repetition, not yet familiar."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'mori-2026-07-26T20:03:24.881Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Just not familiar yet — more reps needed","angle":"\"Stuck on not being familiar yet. Have to keep practising.\" An honest summary is sometimes exactly this short — no dressing up unfamiliarity as something else.","excerpt":{"title":"Back-to-baseline review","reflection":"Worked on forehand and backhand net shots. Lifting to the rear court off both sides. Forehand clear. Stuck on: not familiar yet. Have to keep practising."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'mori-2026-07-20T17:27:55.233Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"After the net shot, I forgot to step back","angle":"All three sticking points are concrete, and one of them is \"after the net shot I didn''t realise I should step back immediately\" — noticing something you left out is harder than noticing something you did badly.","excerpt":{"title":"Back-to-baseline review","reflection":"Net shots, backhand high serve, doubles rotation, clear-smash then quick recovery to defend at the net. Stuck on: racket face not flat on the net shot. On the backhand clear I can''t let the body drive the arm. Same on the high serve — can''t get the body into it. And after a net shot I don''t realise I should step back straight away.","question":"Need to build the basics properly. Want to learn footwork."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'mori-2026-07-17T20:14:28.922Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"The smash I can''t flatten, I hide from in matches","angle":"\"When I want to smash I can''t get it down, and pressing harder puts it in the net, so in matches I avoid the shot altogether.\" Admitting you are avoiding a shot is worth more than a hundred blind smash reps — now the coach knows where to start.","excerpt":{"title":"C1 adult baseline assessment","reflection":"30/05 Split step + body rhythm\n\n1. On the split step I focus on driving off the loading foot; my read of court position is weak.\n2. My feel for generating force through the body is still weak — it feels awkward. During free play after training I lose the feeling completely. Maybe more reps to build the muscle memory.\n3. Clear tightness in the hand on frontcourt shots.\n4. After training it was noticeably more effective to send flat shots back to the opponent''s rear court.","question":"1. How can I get more of the body into the shot?\n2. More footwork work — it sticks right after training but using it under pressure still needs work.\n3. Smash. When I want to smash I clearly can''t get it down, and pressing harder puts it in the net. So in matches I avoid the shot."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'zi-xuan-2026-07-06T08:12:12.826Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"80% straight, 20% cross","angle":"She turned experience into rules with numbers in them: \"80% straight, 20% cross\", \"backhand transition 70% flat contact, 30% slice — don''t over-slice\". A rule with a ratio is usable on court; \"pay a bit more attention\" is not.","excerpt":{"title":"Recover to base within 1 second","reflection":"1. Worked the six-corner footwork. I''m gradually able to use the round-the-head footwork in normal games — must remember to use the waist and core! The footwork is more familiar than before. One thing to watch is generating force through the body at the moment of contact.\n2. Practised forehand and backhand net interceptions. Remember: 1) don''t stand too close to the net, 2) after the first net shot don''t rush in — put the weight back on the left foot and wait to pounce on the next one, 3) watch the cross-court position at the same time and cover it deliberately, 80% straight and 20% cross; if they go cross, try to cut it off in the air.\n4. The clear comes from driving the hips, transferring the body''s force upward; the racket preparation can''t be too loose, and the shape should hold whether it''s a drop or a clear. The contact point matters too — when I''m slightly off, the force doesn''t transfer fully.\n5. On drops, remember to take the shuttle as close to overhead as possible, not up and out to the side.\n6. I also noticed I don''t know how to play the forehand drive under pressure, and a few days without practice loses the feel. The backhand drive has improved; sometimes the distance and contact point still don''t quite line up. When they do, it should come out fairly flat.\n7. For the backhand transition, whether the body faces sideways or fully to the back can come step by step — remember 70% is flat contact and 30% is slice, don''t over-slice.\n8. My lift feels more consistent now; next is making the movement smaller and finding the feel of brushing the strings through the shuttle.\n9. The net shot has definitely steadied — the arm doesn''t wave around any more and I''m not forcing with it. What I''m watching now is adjusting the racket face angle.\n10. I also worked on rotation. I know the basics, but in play I sometimes unconsciously avoid the shuttles I''m not comfortable with — probably a habit from mixed doubles of leaving it to my partner. Women''s doubles is different: I need the most basic, safest option and can''t lean so much on anticipating at the net.","question":"Drive and drop consistency, net kills and the backhand transition; I think the footwork could be trained the way it is for singles."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'sheng-xinyi-2026-07-03T16:04:11.465Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Following through on a clear isn''t braking a smash","angle":"\"After contact you ride the path down — that is different from braking on a smash.\" She worked out for herself the essential difference between clear and smash in the follow-through. The same entry also says \"my backhand is much stronger than before, I can even lift it deep\" — the before-and-after is right there in the writing.","excerpt":{"title":"Serve variation and cushioned smash defence","reflection":"Training summary:\n1. Worked on the push and net kills. It feels easier to handle when the shuttle is slow; remember to generate force from the core, there''s a sense of touching the shuttle in the instant of the rebound. It''s still not natural or reliable for me — I feel like I can''t quite control the direction.\n2. Worked on net kills. If the force comes from the core it is definitely bigger, but the momentum on the braking is bigger too and it feels like I can''t pull it back. I''m also not used to killing a shuttle directly above my head — contact point, power and braking all need more work.\n3. Learned the cross-court net hook for the first time. The grip can be a bit like holding a fly swatter; on the backhand it sits on the bevel. Remember: after dropping the elbow the racket face should be as close to perpendicular to the net as possible, the path follows the elbow drop, and the wrist can control the power — just follow the path of the dropping elbow.\n4. Worked on drops and smashes. Remember that relaxed does not mean a dropped elbow; relaxation belongs in the power phase, not in the preparation — always. Legs and feet find the contact point first; remember my usual point, around one or two o''clock overhead, i.e. the top right. The preparation doesn''t need to be fully extended — set it first, then relax and draw back. Relax through the swing, concentrate the force at contact, then unload with pronation. After contact, link straight into the next shot, recover to base, and turn side-on fully — don''t be lazy.\nWorked footwork: all six of the corners the coach taught. It genuinely needs more review and reps. Going to the net the foot should turn out, not in, and after contact push back immediately with the weight adjusted on that step. The Malaysian step my training partner mentioned — that little push off the middle step — I still can''t feel it, I keep worrying about losing balance.\nWith my training partner I practised the cross-court hook, net kills, and linking the rear-court slice drop and reverse slice. Especially that last one: remember to recover to base and keep it continuous — the feet have to arrive first or the stroke falls apart."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'sheng-xinyi-2026-06-04T06:05:31.933Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Fifteen lessons in one holiday, all of it here","angle":"Fifteen lessons over one holiday, organised into six themes: handling the rear court under pressure, slicing the return of serve, defensive balance, backhand clear, cross-court net hooks, push and lift. The density is close to coach''s notes — plenty of it can be taken straight to the court.","excerpt":{"title":"Closing the net and linking the China jump in the rear court","question":"Repeat and consolidate based on the summary above.","reflection":"Fifteen lessons over the summer holiday. Summary below:\n1. Rear-court trick: against a flat fast shot or a clear that has me under pressure, don''t set the racket up — with the racket low, scooping from below and up gives better power for a clear or a smash. Very effective when pressured at the back.\n2. Return of serve detail: on the sliced return, cock the wrist and extend the arm to take it early, then cut down with the wrist while gripping the fingers, then draw back. Keep the arm straight and let it lead the body, and lower the centre of gravity at the same time.\n3. Defence detail: sit the hips slightly up so the weight is on the balls of both feet with the heels off the floor. Hit with a small hop, keep the elbow low to drive so the shuttle stays flat. For a shot into the low right pocket, hit with the elbow up. On a backhand drive to the right, turn the wrist to load and borrow the pace. Find position by turning the shoulder; the left arm pulls back roughly onto the hip rather than opening out. Grip the fingers, push forward, remember to snap the wrist and don''t go up over the shuttle, so it stays flat.\n4. Backhand clear: adjust the footwork to the tempo, gather the racket slowly to the right with the right hand near the left shoulder — a feeling of compressing the right side of the chest — then drive the elbow up fast, swing and stop, then carry through down the right side. The arm should be straight and it has to stop. Turn the left foot 90 degrees first and push right and up; land the right foot after contact, with the weight moving left to right.\n5. Cross-court net hook: on the forehand, extend the arm straight, don''t bend it; reach the racket out flat first, then turn with the wrist while moving down slightly.\n6. Push and lift: the point is a small movement producing a flat fast shot. Keep the arm straight and hit by gripping the fingers in an instant — don''t bend the arm to load — so the shuttle stays flat. Usable in singles, or in doubles pushed to their backhand rear corner.\n7. Ready position: don''t stick the hips up, sit down into it. After hitting, watch the opponent''s shot and start quickly, but the recovery itself can be slower. Don''t dwell on the quality of your own shot — watch their racket and their movement.\n8. Low backhand hook: turn the shoulder to the landing point, lower the centre of gravity; as the shuttle crosses, tip the racket back, sweep it across flat, then flip the wrist and cock it. The flip has to be fast and travel a bit further, with the arm straight. Overrun it and you slice it — done right there is a clear sound at contact.\n9. Smash: adjust so the shuttle is up to the right; the right foot goes back, the left toe points at me and lifts off. On the smash the left hand points at the incoming shuttle then draws back and gathers up to the right, while the right elbow drops further behind me before being thrown out — get more shoulder into the rotation. If the shuttle is too far behind, hop the right foot back once more before pressing down. If the contact point is too far left and a full smash is awkward, use a stick smash. Watch the rhythm: as the right foot lands, the right shoulder has turned through, sinks, and the left toe turns 180 degrees.\n10. Block at the net: racket up, block to the net, then place the racket where the shuttle crosses; lower the centre of gravity.\n11. Singles: after hitting, walk slowly and above all watch the opponent, not the shuttle — watch the person. Then start quickly as they hit. Singles footwork: open both legs on the split step; for the Malaysian step smash, hop further back then chassé forward.\n12. Singles smash defence: first read the smasher''s action. Against a fast smash, jump and open both feet to start, and keep the reply flat — lock the racket, move forward and lock it there rather than carrying it up, and restrain the freedom of the hand.\n13. Ready position on partner''s serve: stand a bit closer to the centre line and split step on every shot rather than standing still.\n14. Racket up: on the net pounce and on pushing a shot taken below the net, keep the racket up rather than dropping the head.\n15. Backhand low cross into forehand mid-rear footwork drill: after hitting from the forehand mid-rear court, hop so the right foot is in front of the left, cross-step to the backhand hook, chassé back to midcourt, then cross behind for the forehand — and repeat.\n16. Stick smash from the left mid-rear court: step the left leg out to the left, then smash off one leg on the spot; the right hand reaches left from behind the head to adjust the contact point rather than bending the body to find the shuttle. Contact is made by gripping the racket in a fist and pronating roughly 180 degrees, and it should stop at the end, perpendicular to the net — don''t pull the racket back, lock it. Read the shuttle to decide whether to move left, with the weight on the left and the body leaning back.\n17. Doubles rotation drill: at the net, adjust the covering position according to where the partner hits. If she hits straight from the right, shade left; if she smashes straight from the left, shade right, let her come in and move back to the rear court; follow and cover by the shuttle, e.g. a cross-court reply.\n18. Mid-front net-kill drill: crouch, don''t stick the hips up; hold the racket in a fist grip and generate force by pronating, turning the wrist towards the head then forward into contact. Take it further back and stop after contact — not pulling back, but at an angle to the net. To cover a left-hander''s net, lean left with the right side up, but don''t draw the leg in.\n19. Left midcourt low backhand under pressure into right rear court then smash footwork: start from midcourt with the left foot parallel to the net, pull the left hand back and right to drive the right hand left, cross the right foot over, hit, then bring the left foot up keeping the step large. Then watch, push off the left foot to start, take the defensive shot on the right — toes perpendicular to the net — and chassé back. Then set the right foot back with the toe towards the net, weight back, upper body pulling the racket back, and after the smash land on the right foot, push off forward and start for the shuttle on the left of midcourt.\n20. Smash and net-kill footwork drill: after the smash, land on the left foot then jump, rotate the upper body to smash, land the right foot, step the left foot back while pulling the right foot back, raise the racket and kill three times, then two steps right, racket up and turn the wrist to control the direction of the slice into a net shot.\n21. Doubles rear-court side-to-side footwork drill: from the rear left, after turning, the padding step of the right foot should point forward rather than too far back, with the upper body also more towards the back; then push out, land the right foot, chassé to the middle, step the left foot forward-right and the right foot back; after hitting, right foot forward, left foot left, chassé, turn and hit, and repeat.\n22. Backhand drive under pressure: keep the wrist turn fairly flat rather than flipping it upward, tip the racket, flip, then lock the wrist and press with the fingers — including the little finger, which can''t be loose — while staying relaxed. Then the shuttle comes out flat and fast.\n23. Net pounce drill: rise onto both feet and move forward together, racket up and pounce, then hop back on the toes to prepare for the next shot; then a backhand pounce, then draw the left foot back and cover the straight line with the racket up. Use pronation to generate force, not the upper arm. If the pounce is cross-court, chassé across and cover the cross-court front.\n24. Learn to relax.\n25. Going to the net, don''t bring the left foot in too far, so I can pull back quickly.\n26. After hitting from the rear court, cross-step straight forward and put the racket up at the front rather than chasséing or shuffling.\n27. The importance of the left foot: use it more to start. For the left-side net approach, after splitting with both feet open in midcourt, push the left foot towards the centre and travel forward-left rather than simply stepping the right foot forward.\n28. Net footwork drill: if it''s close, jump with both feet and jump back with both; if it''s further, cross-step in and cross-step back.\n29. Backhand power isn''t a direct backward flip of the wrist but a rotation, with the back three fingers gripping the handle in an instant and locking it.\n30. Net kill: after hitting, start with a backward hop rather than a cross-step retreat; the hand has to be up, the weight on the right, the racket tipped back, then pronate rather than staying flat — that way it''s less likely to go into the net. Pronate, then grip.\n31. Coach Xia''s lesson: on the forehand lift, bend the arm and draw a small circle, not a big one; push off the left leg to lift quickly. Right now my left leg isn''t pushing hard enough. Complete the circle before contact so the shuttle goes straight. Don''t bring the left foot up too far — keep the weight balanced and central. After the circle and contact, the racket face should be up, roughly perpendicular to the floor. After a left-handed lift, watch that the fingers don''t loosen — stabilise the racket, I have a habit of dropping it. On footwork, I learned that during the wait the left foot is in the air, and the direction it pushes off controls the direction of the approach.\n32. Coach Xia''s lesson 2: learned the net wipe-pounce and the soft block, and adjusted the footwork. Footwork: going in for a net shot, weight between both legs, then one step back — it has to be stable, and stepping back a bit further leaves space that invites their net shot. Then the pounce footwork: it isn''t upward, and the weight shouldn''t sit too much on the right foot; use both feet together. There should be a slight hang time, then wipe from right to left. On the grip, keep the angle between racket and net small, with the index finger hooked on the handle. Same on both sides. Against fast shots, the soft block: don''t get lazy with the footwork, the grip angle should lean back then move forward and slightly down, and always change between forehand and backhand grips with the racket up. The soft block goes forward and down rather than straight down. Footwork adjustment: for example moving right to left, if I get back too quickly with both feet planted, I have to restart; if the right or left foot is still in the air — with the left foot in the air, push off it directly; with the right foot in the air, place it at the contact point with the weight moving centre or forward, keeping that foot off the ground while the left foot pushes back and chassés forward.\n33. Coach Xia''s lesson 3: grip changes. On the forehand the index finger sits higher than the thumb and the other three fingers can''t be too loose, which reduces instability; on the backhand the thumb sits on the bevel and the racket has to be up. The effect is that straight replies come more easily, it''s less likely to go into the net, the wrist isn''t locked so it''s more flexible, and the racket stays up when recovering rather than dropping. 2. Footwork: when starting to the left, the left foot withdraws to between the feet, then chassé to the net; during the chassé the left leg shouldn''t come too close to the right — it needs to support the weight in the middle rather than on the right leg, which helps me pull back fast. When preparing for a higher shot from the opponent, put the right foot behind the left, which helps the backward start.\n\nI''ve been through all of the above, but I haven''t drilled them enough to use them fluently in matches. Later on I''ll need many more repetitions until they show up in real play."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'yang-jingnan-2026-07-20T07:25:11.017Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Always cross-court and always countered? It''s your toes","angle":"She traced a recurring pattern back to its root cause: \"When they drive to my rear forehand I always go cross-court and get countered — the cause is footwork, my toes should be behind the shuttle.\" Rarer still, the same passage admits \"I have too little variation of my own, I lean heavily on my partner''s net play.\"","excerpt":{"title":"Closing the net and linking the China jump in the rear court","reflection":"Women''s doubles summary: Fan Zheng and I played the Finnish twins Essi and Tiia. A big share of our points came from Fan''s variation at the net and her kills there; fewer came from my smashes at the back — I scored off a smash to the left, one to the right, then a cross or one down the middle. The rest came from fast pushes or moving them into the backhand rear corner. Honestly, I rely on attacking from the back, feeding balls to their backhand rear and pushing to create chances for her to kill at the net — very dependent on my partner changing direction and tempo up front. I have too little variation of my own. One clear pattern: when they drive to my rear forehand I always go cross-court and get countered. The cause is footwork — my toes should be behind the shuttle, not pointing at it. On return of serve I wanted to work on slicing today so I had no real advantage, and I smashed too much from the back while my drops lacked speed and disguise. There were also a few shuttles we both left, though mostly the partnership worked well.","question":"1. Keep refining the smash-and-drop combination. 2. Forehand and backhand drives, both attacking and under pressure. 3. Variation in smash defence."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'yang-jingnan-2026-07-19T18:32:17.061Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"If the reply isn''t deep, you never get back in time","angle":"After the match she wrote out three positioning rules for herself: watch both front corners after serving, and recover to a flat side-by-side base straight after taking a shuttle at the back. Thinking at that level during a match matters more than remembering one technique.","excerpt":{"match":"01/07 Pre-match doubles preparation","experience":"1. Not getting back in time for their reply. Most likely because my own reply lacks depth — it only reaches their midcourt, so their shot comes back too fast. 2. After serving from the front, if I''m not retreating I need to watch both front corners. 3. After taking a shuttle on the back line (usually hitting to their rear court), recover to the middle immediately and hold a flat side-by-side base.","whatWorked":"Continuous rotation and linking shots","nextAdjustment":"I read how threatening their reply is too slowly, so I can''t respond correctly. Movement after my own reply also needs work."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'zi-xuan-2026-07-06T08:01:50.883Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Probe first, then vary, then stay patient","angle":"A doubles match that went well, and what she wrote down was \"spend the first two points probing how strong their rear court is\" and \"if a shot is at all risky, play the safer one\". Being able to explain why you won is rarer than reviewing a loss.","excerpt":{"match":"2026-07-04 Doubles","experience":"1. In doubles a rear-court shot just needs to be in position; you can spend the first two points probing how strong their back court is. 2. Add variation — after a run of rear-court shots, drop one in front. 3. Stay patient and play safe; if a shot is at all risky, leave it and play the safer reply. 4. Hold your focus, and use the gaps between points to reset.","whatWorked":"Played doubles with Zixuan at hobby level — honestly I found it fairly straightforward. 1. Tactically, in doubles you can just lift boldly. As long as the lift is high, deep and in position, they tend to make the error themselves. 2. Then I adjusted the plan to staying patient. Some shuttles I could flatten in one shot, but some of those are risky — take the safe option, no need to gamble. 3. The backhand from training showed up in the match: I drove a few flat and also got some clears out of it. 4. The attacking footwork from training showed up too — round-the-head attacks on both sides felt better the more I played, and I could control their speed. 5. Smash on the chances rather than everything. I smashed a lot early on, which wasn''t necessary; later I picked my moments, which scored more efficiently and saved energy. 6. I also backed my partner up more, covered more ground myself, and talked to her more.","nextAdjustment":"Need to learn to change tempo; watch smashes going into the net or long; and drop-shot consistency, especially the defensive drop while moving."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'sheng-xinyi-2026-07-05T14:12:51.303Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"I need a breather, my partner wants to stay hot","angle":"The hardest thing in mixed doubles isn''t technical: she needs a pause between points to focus, while her partner wants to stay warm. Writing down a tempo mismatch between partners is the only way it ever gets discussed.","excerpt":{"match":"2026-07-04 Mixed doubles","experience":"1. Talk to your partner more. For my own focus I need a short pause between points, but my partner wants to stay hot and doesn''t want to rest. 2. When they push me to the back I can vary more — a soft, deep drop is hard for both of them to take, especially with the woman moving forward, so I should aim more at the seam between them. 3. Play safe: keep the shuttle down but don''t rush the finish, stay patient and be ready for a long rally. 4. In the warm-up, warm up the push more. Find ways to send it over the woman''s head where the man can''t take it comfortably. In normal practice I should work on placement control.","whatWorked":"After playing doubles, coming into mixed I wasn''t sure how to play it at first. Worked on adjusting. 1. At the net I can push softly — no need to fear pushing to the man. As long as I push deep and control the pace so it isn''t fast, it''s still awkward for him. 2. Change tempo. If their man drives well, control the speed so he can''t drive easily. 3. Actually commit to the plan of playing at the woman. 4. Varying the serve worked well; once my head was settled the serve got steadier and I could mix the lines. 5. When they hook me cross-court I don''t have to hook back — read the situation on court. 6. Don''t panic when they attack my rear court; variation works, lifting is the last resort, a drop is best and otherwise keep it down.","nextAdjustment":"1. I forgot to step back and cover the cross when my partner smashed, and they drove a few back. 2. My push quality needs work lately — I''m not controlling height and depth well. 3. I hadn''t played mixed in a while, so I sometimes forgot to follow my partner''s net shot in. Partnership too: after I smash I can''t always get to the net, but after a drop I can — and when I don''t trust my own drop I smash instead, which is where we get out of sync. 4. And keep my own unforced errors down. Again it comes back to patience: no need to take a slightly risky option just to make one shot better."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'sheng-xinyi-2026-07-05T14:45:36.874Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Overheads: how to take them, how to face fewer","angle":"He split the problem in two — \"how to take it\" and \"how to stop them producing it in the first place\". The second half is the valuable one.","excerpt":{"title":"Smash diagnosis + split-step timing","question":"How to handle shots played over my head — I had no answer today. In doubles I deal with their high serve badly, especially on the backhand. In the rally I get lobbed over and often can''t get into position. I want to work on this — how to take it. Or if possible, how to avoid letting them play that shot at all.","reflection":"1) The swing path needs work. Picture a wall on my right side and swing along it, don''t drift left. This is slowly improving. 2) Start earlier — the feeling of \"move the moment they make contact\". Very hard to use in a match: my reading isn''t accurate, the habit is hard to change, and heavy footwork and poor balance mean the intent is there but the body isn''t. Needs more practice. 3) My grip angle is often wrong and needs fixing. I roughly understand the principle — at contact the racket face should be facing forward, otherwise it''s wrong. Needs practice and adjustment to my own body. 4) Doubles covering and movement: when covering for a partner, take the straight side first; when attacking at the net, be more aggressive and quicker. The most obvious sticking points are grip angle and swing path — my nerves can''t adapt to them yet, it feels odd and my shoulder tires faster."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'wang-han-2026-07-02T20:54:11.205Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Lifts fly past the stop, net kills run on a count","angle":"She quantified her own fault: \"the lift should stop at mid-body height, and several times I swung it up to head height.\" Knowing how far off you are is what tells you how far to correct.","excerpt":{"title":"Baseline assessment","reflection":"2026-07-08 Today''s work: drops, lifts, net kills, backhand returns, continuous clears. Before starting, three body movements on the spot: rotating the waist left and right, hitting while leaning back, and driving forward-left into a lunge. Drops: read where the shuttle will land first, get the racket ready and aimed before hitting. Lifts: let the body drive it, keep the hand movement small, and it should finish at mid-body height (several times I swung up to head height). Net kills: bring the racket straight back to neutral after one, count 1, 2 in my head, hold the rhythm and be ready for the next shot. After taking a shuttle don''t stand there waiting — recover quickly and link to the next one. If I get pulled to an extreme corner, recover to the middle promptly; the next shuttle will most likely go the other way."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'zi-xuan-2026-07-10T17:05:33.298Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Doubles isn''t only speed and smashes","angle":"\"On defensive shots secure the quality first, keep net movements small, and make the drop faster and flatter.\" Moving from chasing speed everywhere to knowing which shot should be safe and which should press — that is the dividing line in doubles.","excerpt":{"match":"Doubles group match review","experience":"In doubles you can''t chase only speed and smashes. On defensive shots secure the quality first, keep movements small at the net, and make drops faster and flatter; push the return of serve quicker to pressure their serve first, then widen the attack with slice smashes, slice drops and drop variation.","whatWorked":"Return of serve was decent overall — I can already pressure their serve with the push. There''s attacking intent from the rear court, with the basics of slice smash, slice drop and line variation in place.","nextAdjustment":"1. Strengthen defensive shots on both sides, especially the forehand — about 3 shuttles went straight to the floor today. 2. Drops are slow and high; make them faster and flatter. 3. Slice smash and slice drop used far too little; the shot mix has to be wider than smashing. 4. Push the return of serve faster to pressure their serve. 5. Don''t rush the shot at the net; keep it small to avoid going long. 6. Read the contact point and quality before smashing to avoid netting it or leaving it high to be countered."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'yang-jingnan-2026-05-28T19:45:26.552Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Don''t reach forward for a smash — stand back a little","angle":"A practice list you could set the court up from: mark net-kill targets with objects, adjust your balance to the angle of the smash, and take the smash a little further back instead of reaching in front of you.","excerpt":{"title":"Closing the net and linking the China jump in the rear court","question":"Practice content: 1. Net kill direction changes to different targets — e.g. put a few objects down to mark different spots and practise replying to each. 2. Net kill into a soft block, then retreat with the racket up to take the reply. 3. Weight changes after the smash: a steep smash means a high centre of gravity, a flat smash a low one. 4. Singles smash defence with direction change: change where you take the shuttle — don''t try to take it in front of you, because it''s fast; take it a bit further back, around where it would land, so it''s slower and there''s more reaction time for the footwork. 5. If the sliced return of serve isn''t working, adjust the racket face, point body and toes at the server, then cock the wrist, cut down and lock, then spring the racket back while lowering the centre of gravity. If I can''t get to it high, I can slice upward from underneath. 6. Return of serve drill for rotating the palm — e.g. palm facing left then turning right, or right first then flipping to the backhand facing left. 7. Backhand drive: tip the racket, keep the fingers stable on the handle with a little space, then lock the wrist forward; control straight or cross by the direction of the wrist lock. 8. In singles, if the opponent''s serve is too high, press it softly rather than smashing hard. 9. Net kill: reply by locking with a wrist rotation. Flat and fast can be blocked softly; tight to the net can be wiped. 10. Under pressure in defence, use the weight and the core to spring the shuttle up; practise by lifting the net and driving underneath it. 11. Singles plan: clear first, then slice drop, then clear. 12. Improving the smash: get the racket up fast and high; use the backward pull of the preparation to retreat, increase rotation speed, and take the contact point forward and right for a steeper angle. If there''s no time, hop back a little, meet the shuttle and push up into it, turning on the single right leg. To get the core involved, extend and then crunch forward and down, relax the arm, let the elbow pull further back, use the left arm drawing back, and grip the fingers at contact while the left hand comes up to the right to help the crunch. Then step the right foot further forward. 13. Backhand flat clear drill with stick smashes, combined with a single-leg hop off the left leg or a cross-step — no Malaysian step. 14. Forehand drive drill: how to control straight and cross by adjusting the toes, avoiding always going cross-court. Cross is a longer distance, which lowers placement quality; straight is shorter and gives a better position."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'yang-jingnan-2026-07-21T06:34:52.418Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"The swing got smaller, consistency hasn''t caught up","angle":"\"Smaller movements\" is the result of long-term correction; the same entry also records that blocking and dropping against fast shuttles is still unreliable. Progress and debt written down together — that is a useful review.","excerpt":{"match":"2026-05-09 Mixed doubles","whatWorked":"Good mixed doubles performance, movements got smaller.","nextAdjustment":"Accuracy of the block against fast shuttles and drop-shot consistency both need follow-up."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'student-json:sheng-xinyi:match-review:2026-05-09';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"The arm doesn''t push — the body flows","angle":"\"On the push the body drives the arm; there''s a kind of flowing state and the arm isn''t forcing.\" Being able to describe the sensation of generating force like that means the feel for the kinetic chain is genuinely there.","excerpt":{"title":"Rear-court power","reflection":"A few things I felt in practice today. * On the push, the body drives the arm — there''s a kind of flowing state and the arm isn''t forcing. Also, on the backhand retreat the right foot should be in front, which makes the quick recovery into the next shot easier. * On forehand and backhand lifts, use a whipping action to borrow the pace and generate force from the proximal segments. The backhand is fairly good; the forehand has improved but is still behind the backhand. * The clear action is basically stable now, though sometimes accuracy in finding the shuttle affects the quality. * On the drop the shoulder isn''t relaxed enough. A new feeling is to take it as high as possible and control the racket with the wrist — I''m not sure it''s the right method, but the result is decent. * Linking after the serve has improved and I get to the shuttle better. * Personally I feel my smash, net kill and downward pressure have all improved lately — I''m using the body''s force and generating it more fully. * When I play from the rear court I don''t recover to base well enough, so I often lose the next shot. * The hardest thing right now is that after a net shot I mostly fail to follow in and cover the net; I habitually retreat after the net shot and need to find a way to change the habit."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'xue-meijiao-2026-07-31T19:13:31.447Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Why the forehand doesn''t flow like the backhand","angle":"Lift, drive, smash — the forehand runs less smoothly than the backhand on all of them, and she named the training direction herself. The same entry comes with a very detailed set of notes on generating power in the clear.","excerpt":{"title":"Clear depth control","question":"The forehand needs work — on lifts, drives and smashes it all runs less smoothly than the backhand.","reflection":"A summary of today''s training. 1. Clear: drive the hips first, the upper body is thrown out after, and the arm doesn''t force. Use the body to find the shuttle, watch the shape when the arm stops, and keep the whole body structure balanced and stable. 2. Drive: brush the shuttle, keep it flat, use the body''s force, and bring the weight back quickly. The backhand is better than the forehand. On the forehand I can''t find the contact point, so I can''t use the body''s force and don''t travel through the shuttle, which makes a straight reply hard. 3. Drop: hold the preparation shape, use the body to find the shuttle. Body and arm still aren''t loose and easy enough. Overall I can keep errors fairly low, but the quality can improve further. 4. Movement: retreating after a lift is basically there; following in after a net shot needs work. When my partner takes a shuttle at the back, move up promptly to cover the net; when she lifts, retreat to a flat side-by-side base. I understand it in theory but need more repetition in real play."},"coachFeedback":"Meijiao — I''ve read the summary you sent in. Conclusion first: the underlying logic of your movement and tactics is your biggest recent improvement, and you can now get your rear-court power into a match. Those two I''m only confirming, not re-teaching.\n\nIn yesterday''s doubles movement work your sense of tempo and position were both good — when to move up, when to drop back, where you sit relative to your partner. You were following the logic rather than being dragged around by the shuttle.\n\nBack in May you wrote two things yourself. One was \"I understand the movement in theory but need more repetition in real play\" — real play has now caught up. The other was \"following in after a net shot needs work\" — that one is still open. In matches you sometimes still back away after playing the net shot.\n\nHere''s a test that works better than \"remember to follow in\": whether you follow depends on how good that net shot was. If it''s tight to the tape and dropping as it crosses, so they can only lift or barely reach it, you must follow in and hold that position — that''s what makes the net shot pay. Retreating there wastes it. If the shot goes high or floats, don''t follow; recover to the middle and get ready for the pounce.\n\nSo this isn''t a habit problem. It''s whether, in the instant after you play it, you read the quality of your own shot. Yesterday''s session showed the positional sense is already there; what''s missing is that read-then-decide switch. Watch for it in your next match: when the pace picks up, do you just retreat?"}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'xue-meijiao-2026-07-06T19:19:59.942Z';

update public.student_history_records set featured_en = '{"title":"Not rotating is when the round-the-head is fastest","angle":"She merged several lessons into one entry: buying time in the round-the-head corner by not rotating, warming up from small net shots outward to bigger movements, and the push living in the instant the weight snaps back. Very dense — worth reading slowly.","excerpt":{"title":"Serve variation and cushioned smash defence","reflection":"A summary of the last few lessons, put together:\n1. On footwork, the rear court feels much smoother and it shows up in matches, especially in the round-the-head corner. I can imagine being attacked behind me — not rotating is when I''m fastest, so I should learn to use the non-rotating version to play the round-the-head. Generating power that way is harder, and I should be thinking about several variations: the high clear, the flat clear and the drop. Generally lifting it high is better in doubles; in mixed I can put in a drop, but the quality has to be there — I can''t smash lightly, because if it comes back at me I''m immediately on the defensive.\n2. The warm-up can go from small to large, which carries over to everyday practice: start with net shots. I''ve found these are far more consistent than before — the arm just sits there without forcing, feeling the shuttle bounce and roll back off the strings. A stable arm makes for a more stable reply.\n3. Then the push, both the soft push to the midriff and the deep push, both generated by the body. I need to watch the force in the instant the weight snaps back, especially pressing the weight down a little. I can feel the body giving a small shudder of force, and the arm now stays stable without forcing — though when the pace picks up I may stop paying attention. The backhand feels easier to play than the forehand.\n4. Then drives, forehand and backhand, driving to the baseline. Give the arm the maximum range and don''t sacrifice the space between arm and body. Then the forehand''s body-generated force, which is bigger than the above — the more force, the more relaxed the arm has to be, that''s what gives the whipping feel. The backhand has a feeling of pushing towards the side of the court, so the arm can contract slightly first and then push out sideways; the force towards the opposite court doesn''t need managing, it comes out naturally. My backhand feels much stronger than before — I can even lift it deep. I just have to control the direction; keeping it flatter means watching the power. For now it''s fine if practice shots go long.\n5. On the clear the arm has to be relaxed. Rotating or not doesn''t actually affect the power — what matters is transferring the body''s force and concentrating it. Also keep the preparation stable after setting up and don''t drop the racket; being too relaxed can''t be the reason it falls. Stay stable before contact, then relax the arm with the shoulder steady. After contact, ride the path down — different from braking on a smash. The smash demands more of the linking movement.\n6. Same for the drop: whether it''s a slice drop or a reverse slice, both can be done without turning, and the power can be the same. I can start by feeling the version with less rotation. Now I want to place it where I aim, and sometimes I slice too much and it goes into the net — worth watching. The contact point can be closer to overhead rather than out in front, which makes netting it less likely. Feel the low-rotation version first; adding rotation later is really for consistency of action.\n7. Now I''ve learned a new way of generating force by brushing the strings. I can wait until I''ve read the shuttle before hitting — same for the lift, the drop, and the drive; anything with more time and space works. I haven''t mastered it yet; in training with the coach guiding I can produce it, and I''m still trying, though sometimes I forget.\n8. At the net, be clear about which incoming shuttles can be pressed down, which need a soft touch, and which are so defensive they must be lifted. In principle, if it comes tight over the tape and I can take it near the net cord, I can wipe it; if it''s lower, try a net shot or a soft push; only when really under pressure do I lift. But I need to pin the specifics down more.\n9. I feel the clear needs more work too — I''m always so relaxed that I drop the racket, and I haven''t got the balance right. And the core has to be tight, generating force from the waist and abdomen. The backhand transition is also only just being learned. Right now consistency on a lot of shots needs practice."}}'::jsonb
  where external_id = 'sheng-xinyi-2026-06-29T08:36:50.966Z';

